The null key
description: "'~: v' and ': v' — a keyed entry whose key is the null value"
- >
  Adopted from YAML (the YAML-keys round, 2026-08-01): a mapping key may be the **null value**. `: v`
  ≡ `~: v` is a **keyed** entry whose key is null — distinct from the keyless `- v`, which holds only
  a position, and from `'': v`, the empty-*string* key. Canonical emission is `~: v`; in a path the
  null key is addressed by the bare `~` portion (`: doc: ~`). A literal tilde key is written quoted or
  escaped — `'~': v` in data, `\~` inside a longer path portion.
- !!yo
  doc:
    ~: the null-keyed entry      # the same entry as ": the null-keyed entry"
    '~': a literal-tilde key     # the STRING key "~"
    - a keyless entry            # position only — no key at all
- >
  The adoption has deliberate boundaries. `null: v` stays the **string key** `null` — the
  keys-are-strings doctrine: a *word* in key position is a string, the way a plain numeric key `1: v`
  is a position claim and a parse error ([keys are
  pointers](::language:principles:keys-are-pointers)). Both are documented YAML divergences, ruled
  together.
- >
  json5p has **no null-key spelling** — JSON5 object keys are strings, so serializing a null-keyed
  entry raises a LossyError. Anchors may not create the null key — `&: p: ~` is rejected; the null key
  is authored in place ([anchors](::language:pointers:anchors)). And the sigil reading lost this
  collision: a back-edge needs a nonempty name, so a bare `~: v` line is always the null-keyed entry,
  never an empty-named back-edge ([the tilde sigil](::language:vs-yaml:tilde)).
